 "findings": [
  {
   "evidence": "The definition is `def op (c : CrossedSystem k D G) : CrossedSystem k D\u1d50\u1d52\u1d56 G` with `factor g h := Units.opEquiv.symm (MulOpposite.op (c.factor g h)\u207b\u00b9)`. Its own docstring says 'with inverse unit factors. The index monoid is unchanged.' The module header calls these 'inverse factors', separate from 'opposite coefficients'.",
   "file": "TauCeti/Algebra/CrossedProduct/Noncommutative/Opposite.lean",
   "fix": "Rename it to a name that states both changes, e.g. `CrossedSystem.opInv`, and rename `op_action`/`op_factor` to match (`opInv_action`, `opInv_factor`). Keep `op` free for a true opposite system indexed by `G\u1d50\u1d52\u1d56`.",
   "issue": "In Mathlib, `X.op` (`AlgEquiv.op`, `RingHom.op`, `MulOpposite.op`) means the opposite of `X`, so `c.op` reads as the opposite crossed system, whose algebra would be the opposite algebra. That algebra would be indexed by `G\u1d50\u1d52\u1d56`, with action and factors coming from reversing the multiplication. This definition keeps `G`, applies `\u03c3_g.op` and uses `op(a(g,h)\u207b\u00b9)`, so a consumer will wrongly expect `c.op.Algebra \u2243\u2090 (c.Algebra)\u1d50\u1d52\u1d56`. The name hides the factor inversion, which is the defining feature of the system.",
   "line": 43
  }
 ],
